From d0e5e93e49e514b003db99a7f0981a73e0fae3cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Casey <4348807+justincasey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 11:46:27 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Mixed-K MoE decode on one exl3_moe_coop_mk launch per stage

Layers whose (Kg, Ku, Kd) widths span two adjacent K (<= 4) now decode on a single
exl3_moe_coop_mk call (exllamav3 m31-coop-mixedk) instead of one exl3_moe_coop
launch per K group (patch 0011's split, chained through sh_out). Experts are
renumbered in ascending K-tuple order at build time (pointer tables and K arrays
permuted, router ids mapped at decode), so the kernel, which takes runs in
descending expert order, starts the heavy experts first. VLLM_EXL3_COOP_MK=0
keeps the per-group split for A/B; same batch bound as the split.

GB10, 2.15 pack TP2 rank, live decode routing, per-layer coop time:
2-group layers c=1 466 -> 436 us, c=4 978 -> 941 us; 3-group layers c=1 529 ->
486 us, c=4 1112 -> 999 us. Accuracy vs an fp32 reference unchanged (6.4e-4..
6.6e-4, same as the split).
